{"id":"perks-disclosed","title":"Perks, severance and loans the returns disclose","tier":"REDFLAG","question":"Which City-paid organizations answered yes on their tax returns to first-class travel, housing, club dues, tax gross-ups, personal services, severance or loans involving officers, and with what amounts?","finding":"21 organizations answered yes at least once to one of the eight listed benefits: first-class or charter travel (10), travel for companions (6), tax gross-up payments (4), a discretionary spending account (2), a housing allowance or residence (7), payments for business use of a personal residence (1), health or social club dues (6) and personal services such as a driver or chef (4). 67 reported a severance or change-of-control payment and 25 a supplemental retirement plan; 10 show $5,000 or more owed to the organization by its officers or other insiders and 29 money lent to it by them. Where the City supplied a quarter or more of revenue, 3 organizations disclose a listed benefit (Homeless Children's Network, Alive & Free and Glide Foundation), 14 a severance payment and 19 a balance with officers or other insiders. Those organizations are listed first.","how":"From Schedule J, Part I of every return on file: line 1a (first-class or charter travel, travel for companions, tax indemnification and gross-up payments, a discretionary spending account, a housing allowance or residence, payments for business use of a personal residence, health or social club dues, personal services) and line 4 (severance or change-of-control payment, supplemental non-qualified retirement plan, equity-based pay), with the tax years of each yes. The severance figure is the largest dollar amount that the return’s own explanation of line 4a ties directly to the words severance or separation, where it gives one; settlements and retirement-plan payouts described in the same note are left out. Balances with officers and other insiders are the largest year-end figure on the balance sheet (Part X, line 5, receivables from officers, directors and key employees, and line 22, loans from them) or on the schedule of loans to and from interested persons (Schedule L, Part II); under $5,000 is ignored. The amount for benefits is the largest dollar figure in the return’s explanation of line 1a, where it gives one. Ranked with organizations for which the City supplied at least 25% of revenue on the latest return first, then by the number of different benefits. Where the organization’s tax number was matched by hand and has not been verified, the last column says so.","limits":"Every item here is a disclosure the form requires; the question is whether the board approved it and it was reported as pay where the tax rules say so. A housing allowance can be a condition of the job: one return here explains that its director is required to live on the premises. Several returns say first-class travel is allowed only on long flights, and a severance payment is usually owed under a contract. A loan from an officer can be an officer helping the organization through a cash shortage, and at a lender a loan to a director’s own organization can be ordinary programme lending; the balance-sheet lines also take in insiders other than officers, and Schedule L names the person and the purpose. The schedule follows from the return’s question whether anyone was paid over $150,000, so smaller organizations are not asked these questions. The largest named amount may be a total for several people.","columns":[{"k":"org","t":"Organization","type":"org"},{"k":"perks","t":"Benefits answered yes (tax years)","type":"text"},{"k":"other","t":"Severance, retirement plan or equity (tax years)","type":"text"},{"k":"severance","t":"Largest severance amount named","type":"usd"},{"k":"owed","t":"Most owed by officers or other insiders at a year end","type":"usd"},{"k":"lent","t":"Most lent by officers or other insiders at a year end","type":"usd"},{"k":"perkAmount","t":"Largest amount named for those benefits","type":"usd"},{"k":"cityShare","t":"City share of revenue, latest return","type":"pct"},{"k":"cityPaid","t":"City paid that year","type":"usd"},{"k":"ret","t":"Source","type":"link"},{"k":"matched","t":"Tax record matched","type":"text"}],"rows":[{"org":"Homeless Children's Network","orgKey":"HOMELESS CHILDREN S NETWORK","matched":"","perks":"payments for business use of a personal residence (2021, 2022, 2023, 2024, 2025); health or social club dues (2021, 2022, 2023, 2024, 2025)","other":null,"severance":null,"owed":null,"lent":null,"perkAmount":null,"cityShare":0.8873,"cityPaid":11300758,"ret":{"t":"Return","u":"https://projects.propublica.org/nonprofits/organizations/943266686/202601359349313865/full"}},{"org":"Alive & Free","orgKey":"ALIVE FREE","matched":"","perks":"a discretionary spending account (2023, 2024, 2025)","other":null,"severance":null,"owed":null,"lent":null,"perkAmount":null,"cityShare":0.3057,"cityPaid":655431,"ret":{"t":"Return","u":"https://projects.propublica.org/nonprofits/organizations/943171846/202620419349301922/full"}},{"org":"Glide Foundation","orgKey":"GLIDE FOUNDATION","matched":"","perks":"a housing allowance or residence (2023, 2024)","other":"a severance or change-of-control payment (2024, 2025); a supplemental retirement plan (2023, 2024)","severance":90905,"owed":null,"lent":null,"perkAmount":25544,"cityShare":0.2853,"cityPaid":8078021,"ret":{"t":"Return","u":"https://projects.propublica.org/nonprofits/organizations/941156481/202601359349312970/full"}},{"org":"Project Wreckless","orgKey":null,"matched":"","perks":null,"other":null,"severance":null,"owed":null,"lent":584180,"perkAmount":null,"cityShare":1.5,"cityPaid":488835,"ret":{"t":"Return","u":"https://projects.propublica.org/nonprofits/organizations/475087080/202433199349309488/full"}},{"org":"Bridge Regional Partners Inc","orgKey":"BRIDGE REGIONAL PARTNERS","matched":"","perks":null,"other":"a severance or change-of-control payment (2021, 2022, 2023,